WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:04.799
It's absolutely true that school makes people
show up, sit down, shut up

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.540
and that these are useful skills for people
to have in adulthood.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.840
So the real question is if all we're trying
to do is prepare people for a job,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.360
why not prepare them with a job?

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.100
I'm Nick Gillespie for Reason

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.020
and today we are talking with the author

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.880
of what is almost certainly going to be the
most controversial book of the year.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.740
Bryan Caplan is an economics professor at
George Mason University,

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.680
and his new book is The Case Against Education.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.360
Bryan, thanks for talking with Reason.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.400
Thanks for such an exciting introduction.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.560
Well, let's get right to it.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.440
Early on you say flatly, you write flatly,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.480
"This book argues that our education system
is a big waste of time and money."

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.620
And now you're not simply saying that our
schools are overpriced and uneven in quality,

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.100
you are actually making the case that much
of our traditional education system,

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.680
especially higher ed, is literally a waste
of time, right?

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:55.300
Absolutely.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.200
What do you mean by that?

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.300
What I mean is that people are going there
to get a higher income,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.960
but they're actually not getting much in the
way of job skills,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.680
which raises a big puzzle for an economist.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.260
How can they be getting a higher income if
they're not getting much in the way of job skills?

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.620
And my answer comes down to something called
the signaling model of education

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.400
that says that a lot of the reason why education
pays isn't that you learn useful skills,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.620
but that you distinguish yourself.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.180
That you're getting stamped or labeled.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.760
You're getting a sticker on your forehead,
Grade A worker.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.280
So it's kind of like you come out as a piece
of steak.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.120
You're USDA prime, but you haven't been cooked
yet.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.460
Precisely.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.130
And the the key thing about this is, selfishly
speaking,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.000
it doesn't really matter why you're getting
more money.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.140
But from a social point of view, from the
point of view of is this a good use of taxpayer dollars,

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.740
it matters tremendously because everybody
just gets more years in education

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.080
and all you're doing is showing off.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.580
Then you're just raising the bar for how much
school you need

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.119
in order to get a job in the first place.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.420
So let's talk about the magnitude of wasted
time and money.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.200
I guess for most people, if you go from kindergarten
through a B.A.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.220
you're talking about 17 years roughly.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:01.120
So that's a lot of time, but how much money
do we spend as a society on education?

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.520
Yeah, so government funding is about a trillion
dollars a year when you add up all levels.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.819
Our economy is about 20 trillion dollars,
so that's a large chunk of change.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.480
Yeah, so you're talking about something like
5 percent of all GDP.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.459
And then of course private spending tops it
up a bit further,

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.480
so it's something over a trillion.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.080
And by the way, the one thing that's interesting
about this

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:24.060
is you don't really make a distinction between
private and public education,

00:02:24.069 --> 00:02:29.861
because this isn't a book about how the public
school system is failing kids.

00:02:29.861 --> 00:02:32.920
It's just how education is useless.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.800
I mean, I know libertarians want to hear a
different story,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.879
but I'm telling the story that I think is
true

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.060
rather than the story that I would even find
ideologically most congenial.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.880
For me, the main thing is I've gone to public
school.

00:02:44.889 --> 00:02:45.889
I've gone to private school.

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:47.340
I don't really see very much difference.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.620
And in both cases it seems to me that most
of what's going on

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:53.659
is showing off in order to look better for
the labor market.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.520
Which, again, individually makes perfect sense,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.800
but socially speaking, everyone can't be above
average.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:05.420
So this is kind of like a private vice is
actually becomes a public vice because-

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.299
Or rather a private virtue becomes a public
vice because-

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:08.920
Yeah.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.180
I mean, I think of education as being a lot
like football stadiums,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.380
where like the main libertarian complaint
about football stadiums

00:03:15.389 --> 00:03:17.510
isn't that we're not making the right kind
of football stadiums.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.420
It's that government is pouring a ton of money
on something,

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.200
and really what we need is to have fewer worse
football stadiums.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.740
But more money left to for people to spend
in a meaningful way.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:29.360
Yeah.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.480
But possibly not on football stadiums.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.160
Possibly on totally different things.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.080
Before we go into the signaling model per
se,

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.919
talk a little bit about the human capital
model of education

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.100
because these are the two big competing explanations.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.440
What is the human capital education model?

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.620
Yeah, the human capital model is a fancy phrase

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:53.120
for what your teachers and politicians and
your parents have been telling you,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.519
which is you go to school to get smart and
to learn all kinds of useful stuff

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.040
which you're then going to apply in the real
world.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.260
You're investing in yourself.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.320
Yeah, you're investing in yourself.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:07.660
So you're transforming unskilled laborer into
a skilled, talented adult.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.300
It’s most associated with Gary Becker.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.680
You know, a famous Nobel prize winning economist.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.620
But really he was just putting an academic
veneer

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:20.000
on something that is widely propagandized
in favor of the idea of school as a skills factory.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.259
It's not simply ... I mean, very few people,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.760
and I mean, you point this out continuously
in the book and I think accurately,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.700
nobody if you go to take a shop class,

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:34.380
you're not going to go and learn how to cut
ninja stars out of sheets of metal

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.060
and that's the job you're going to have in
a factory.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.759
But the idea is that you learn skills.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.240
That you learn how to show up on time.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.060
You learn how to listen and follow instructions.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.460
You learn how to self-learn.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.020
And you're saying that this is actually not
a very-

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.640
Like how do you know that that's not an accurate
model.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.580
Right, that's one of the best arguments in
favor of the education we have.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.640
It's one by the way

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.060
that people usually have to be forced into
after a series of intellectual retreats.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:01.060
But, you know, here's the main thing that
I say about that.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:05.680
It's absolutely true that school makes people
show up, sit down, shut up

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.440
and that these are useful skills for people
to have in adulthood.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.680
So the real question is if all we're trying
to do is prepare people for a job,

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.340
why not prepare them with a job instead of
with school where the overlap-

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.180
There is a partial overlap in skills,

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.979
but there of course is a lot of stuff that
you're taught in school

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.160
that is dysfunctional in the real world,

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.220
like it's very important for everything to
be fair.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.340
Jobs aren't fair, but school, everything has
to be fair.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.880
Talk about the sheepskin effect,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.200
because for me, this is the thing that totally

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:36.259
whatever affection I had for the human capital
model of education,

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.240
this really kind of kicked that to the curb.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.960
So diplomas used to be written on the skins
of sheep

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.320
and the sheepskin effect refers to the fact

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:48.200
that a lot of the payoff from education comes
from crossing the finish line,

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.060
from graduation, a disproportionate amount.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.180
So like in the book, I just average over a
whole lot of studies.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:00.700
So as for college, finishing senior year pays
something like seven times as much as a regular year.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:01.740
Seven times as much.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.099
Now either we save almost all the useful job
skills for graduation year,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.440
which sounds really implausible.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.970
You know, your senior year is goof-off year,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.030
not finally-learn-some-job-skills year.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:15.000
Or there's something going on in the rewards
for education

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.380
that's not about skills that you're learning.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:21.020
And I say, like the main thing that we're
getting is it's a signal of conformity.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.980
In our society, you are expected to graduate.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.620
Everyone tells you to graduate.

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:28.780
If you fail to do so despite all of this social
pressure,

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.820
you're saying something very bad about yourself
and the labor market responds negatively to you.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:36.980
Although one of the things that I think is
really interesting about the book

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.960
is that you do steer clear of extremes because
there are-

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.690
Immediately you can hear people saying,

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:42.949
"Well, what about Steve Jobs?

00:06:42.949 --> 00:06:43.760
What about Bill Gates?"

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:44.440
Of course.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:45.940
"What about Michael Dell?"

00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:47.180
There's the list of-

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.160
Or Andrew Carnegie for that matter.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:56.210
So I mean, you're not saying that all of these
things are infinite extremely perfectly, right?

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.499
Yeah, no, of course.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.580
So I have two chapters that are the most quantitative
part

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.720
where I sit around trying to crunch the numbers

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.920
and say what fraction of the payoff for education
comes from human capital,

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:09.240
what fraction comes from signaling.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.120
Of course, you learn reading, and writing,
and math in school to some degree.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.120
And of course those are useful job skills.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.580
But what fraction of the time that you're
in school

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:21.020
is really learning anything you're ever going
to use again?

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.319
What fraction of the payoff comes from what
you've learned

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:27.200
rather than what you've demonstrated you can
do?

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.180
What do you do with the arguments that, well,

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:34.580
school maybe or education and particularly
K-12 education is not for job skills,

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:35.940
but it's for citizenship.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.800
And this is- You know, it's-

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:41.520
The progressive era talked a lot about that,
like we need to make good citizens,

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.509
especially out of increased numbers of immigrants

00:07:43.509 --> 00:07:46.260
who had no understanding of American history.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.680
Where does that fall in the human capital
model?

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:49.200
Right.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.820
So I mean that would actually be totally outside
of it.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:52.960
Again, like human capital versus signaling

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.620
are trying to figure out why do employers
pay you more for it.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.900
Now this other stuff, I have a full chapter
on it as well,

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.860
and it just begins with saying, look, the
argument in principle is sound,

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.409
but we have to look empirically to see whether
it's really true.

00:08:06.409 --> 00:08:07.529
You know, I have a big section

00:08:07.529 --> 00:08:10.870
where I just go over how much civics do American
adults even know?

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:13.340
And the answer is next to nothing.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:14.400
Next to nothing.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.999
Yeah, I mean, you note that if people who
were born here or were citizens

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.500
had to pass the citizenship test of immigrants

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.880
I think was something like 70 percent would
fail.

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:24.889
I'm glad you remember that.

00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:25.889
I don't.

00:08:25.889 --> 00:08:28.949
So what we can see is that like even the most
basic stuff,

00:08:28.949 --> 00:08:32.560
like how many senators does each state have,
maybe half of American adults know this.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.570
And this is not after a one-week course in
civics.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:37.960
So, you know, like you say, well,

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.560
American high school students on average will
do three years of civics and history.

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.480
So three years and yet what do we have to
show for it?

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:46.300
Next to nothing.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:47.680
Well, we have football stadiums.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:48.980
Yes, we have football stadiums.

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:52.040
So talk about the signaling model.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:53.120
How does the signaling model work?

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:57.600
You say partly it shows that you're willing
to conform to certain basic norms

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.120
that are going to make you appealing to employers,

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.900
but there's more to it than that, right?

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.640
Yeah so I mean, there's a lot of different desirable
traits

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.460
that you are signaling with educational accomplishment.

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:07.860
So there's the obvious ones.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:08.940
You're signaling that you're smart.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.140
Smart people do better in school.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:13.560
If you've done well in school, natural inference
is the person's probably smart.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:15.520
Not necessarily, but generally.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.900
You're signaling work ethic because even the
smartest person in the world

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.839
can't do well in school if they don't show
up and do a bit of work.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.080
And then finally, the most subtle one that
I talk about a lot is conformity,

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.980
signaling you're willing to submit to social
norms.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:34.100
And the conformity one is where we really
get things like the sheepskin effect.

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.420
Because if we were just signaling intelligence
and work ethic,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.460
there still isn't really a good story, well,
why does the last year pay so much?

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.880
Once you accept that a lot of it is about,
yes, master,

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:46.660
I will conform to what our society demands
of me, that's where graduation is so important.

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:50.860
That's why in a country where college lasts
three years, it's the third year that's crucial.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.320
In a country where it lasts four years the fourth year is crucial.

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:55.820
Just like in a country where suits are the
standard thing you wear to an interview,

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:00.220
you better wear a suit or else you look like
a weirdo and people don't want to hire you.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:03.400
Libertarianism is about individualism,

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:08.560
but then is there a sliding scale of when
you get too conformist,

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.760
because then you're also not a good worker,
right, in many ways?

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.000
Like you're an economics professor.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.600
If you were simply doing what everybody else
was doing, that would be a problem.

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:22.630
A line that I quote in the book roughly is
what employers want is intelligent conformism.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:27.740
They want people who apply their full intellectual
power to the task that is given to them.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:32.140
Now sometimes there are creative occupations
where they're told be creative on this task.

00:10:32.149 --> 00:10:36.089
Well, even there, almost no employer wants
you to be so creative that you say,

00:10:36.089 --> 00:10:38.910
"Hey, maybe this project isn't even worth
doing, maybe I should be the boss."

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:39.959
So there's always that.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.840
And then again of course most jobs are not
really very creative

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.740
and there's a tendency in the information
age to focus on the small share of jobs

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:52.200
where we do want people shooting basketball
while they shoot the breeze.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:53.800
But I mean, most jobs are not like that.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.040
Vast majority of jobs are not like that.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:56.040
It's about there's a customer.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.560
He wants a definite product, give him that
product or else I don't want you around.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:06.340
Talk about one of the ways that this system
gets enforced is through social desirability bias.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:10.180
What is that and how does that inform the
larger education network?

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:13.500
Social desirability bias is the concept in
psychology

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:17.500
that is barely known by either economists
or libertarians

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:24.300
and yet should be the single most cited concept
in psychology, social desirability bias.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:28.600
You know, a simple version is people like
to say what sounds good.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:32.839
They like to say things that will make people
think that they are a kind and-

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.640
A kind, respectful, and respectable person,
right?

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:40.320
So you can see this in things like what is
the socially desirable answer to, "Am I fat?"

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:43.100
Of course the socially desirable answer is,
"I'm not fat."

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:45.640
Now of course some people aren't fat and then
you just tell them the truth,

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:48.960
but on the other hand, we know that if someone
is in fact fat,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.380
our strong temptation is to say something
that sounds good but isn't true.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.040
This is something where of course we see it
in daily life, but also has clear political roles.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.820
You know, just think about any time a politician
says,

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:03.240
"And we need to put more resources into education,
health care, and the environment."

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:04.790
Now these are all things that sound good.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:06.800
And if you could imagine a politician saying,

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.020
"We have now done enough for education, and
health care, and the environment.

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:12.900
We know they're important, but enough is enough."

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:15.709
That's nothing that a politician would ever
want to stick their neck out and say

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:16.840
because it sounds bad.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:20.740
It sounds like you're not a caring person,
you're not a respectable person.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:26.880
What I say is a lot of the support for education
is social desirability bias in a sense that if we-

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:31.840
The only thing that a good person would say
is more and better, never less and worse.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.760
There are obviously entrenched interests

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:39.520
in a kind of education industry as well as
it helps employers, right?

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:44.020
Because employers, even if we're not learning
the skills,

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:46.840
they're going to train us on the job for whatever
we need to do,

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:51.840
but they benefit in a way, right, from the
signaling process because it makes it-

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:57.720
You at one point you say that employers can't
look at every individual applicant closely,

00:12:57.720 --> 00:12:59.740
so they use these as rough sorts.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:02.440
Yeah, I mean, employers benefit from there
being some signal.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.240
But I don't think employers benefit from the
college degree being the signal of quality

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.670
rather than the high school degree.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:14.120
So something where like in 1950 like about
something like 25 percent of American adults

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:17.339
would have finished high school at that point,
and then an employer could say,

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:19.300
"Well, they're a high school graduate. Great. Perfect.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.780
They're managerial material."

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:21.920
Now it's a college degree.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.900
As to why employers benefit from pulling four
years worth of labor off the job market,

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:28.840
I don't think that they do actually.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:30.480
Can we talk about that?

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:35.740
What is the historical, the kind of material
basis for this?

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:41.900
You know, again, schools became at least up
through sixth grade or eighth grade for most kids,

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:44.640
sort of became mandatory in the early 19th
century.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.740
By the end of the century it was everywhere.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:51.440
Is part of this, is part of the growth of
education as being so important

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.580
and central to our identity,

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.180
is it simply we need to warehouse kids

00:13:55.180 --> 00:14:00.480
now that we don't need them to be chimney
sweeps or to do like things,

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:01.610
you know, kids have little hands.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:05.540
They can work on machines with little hands,
things like that, or hawk newspapers.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.360
We don't need them to do that labor anymore.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:08.820
Are we just warehousing kids?

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:13.960
Like what's the sociology or the genealogy
of why we have so much education?

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:16.700
Going 100 years back, there is this popular
story

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.520
that employers wanted kids trained to be cogs
in the corporate machine.

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:23.420
And I would say like if you really wanted
to train cogs in your corporate machine,

00:14:23.421 --> 00:14:25.910
you would not design anything like the public
school system we have.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:27.500
It would be like military school.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.960
You would whip kids into shape, get them to
say, "Yes, sir."

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.300
Give them a lot of propaganda about how great
their corporate pay masters are.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.880
That's not the way that education looked 100
years ago,

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.480
and it's certainly not the way that it looks
today.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:46.040
This is not a system that really seems to
be designed to prepare people to be useful employees.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.700
You'd never have the everyone's a beautiful
unique snowflake kind of propaganda,

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:50.580
the touchy-feeliness.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:52.480
That's not what employers want.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:59.100
Employers want someone that will follow orders,
do what they're told, and accept criticism,

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.720
which is of course crucial for learning.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.920
So I don't think that it really makes much
sense to think of the current system

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.500
as something that has been molded for the
interest of corporate America.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:10.320
I mean, I think, you know, so if corporate
America

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:14.320
of course if they can tweak it a little bit
in their favored direction they will, but it's-

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:15.780
But what else would we do with kids?

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.139
Yeah, so there's kids and kids, right?

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:21.080
Young kids, of course, they need to be warehoused.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.200
So give them day care.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:25.380
Well, even there, as to why kids can't go
to school

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:27.740
and then learn reading, writing, and math
for a few hours

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:31.800
and then get to play for the rest of the day
within a supervised facility,

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:34.110
I've got no clue about why you couldn't just
do that

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:37.540
rather than boring them to death and making
them study stuff they don't care about.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:41.040
But even for older kids, like 13-year-olds,
14-year-olds, 15-year-olds,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:44.820
as to why they can't actually be out in the
real world as apprentices.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:46.000
There are countries that do this.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.440
Germany and Switzerland do this.

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:50.720
No reason why American kids could not do this
as well, right?

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:52.620
And say like they can't do anything.

00:15:52.629 --> 00:15:53.629
Sure they can do stuff.

00:15:53.629 --> 00:15:54.940
Say, well, we don't need them.

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:57.269
Well, the economy's not based upon what we
need.

00:15:57.269 --> 00:15:59.770
It's based upon what we got, right?

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:00.580
What do we need?

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.680
We need like a few bowls of rice a day.

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:07.500
But what we've got, however, is enough to
go and produce vastly more

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:12.980
and like I don't see any reason why teenagers
could not be part of the labor market

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:15.020
at a much earlier age than they are right
now, right?

00:16:15.029 --> 00:16:18.759
And, you know, of course if we're always worried
that any kid who's working

00:16:18.759 --> 00:16:23.350
is being distracted from his much more important
studies, this isn't going to fly.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:26.870
But if we realize these studies are not really
that socially valuable anyway

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.490
and it would be better to get kids in the
labor force—

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.339
especially, by the way, kids that are not
very academically inclined anyway

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:36.280
where they get shoved and prodded to go and
succeed and go to college,

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:39.100
which is almost certainly never going to happen
for them.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:42.700
And by the time that they drop out, they're
so bitter about the whole system

00:16:42.709 --> 00:16:45.560
that they are not suited for really any job.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:47.550
I've got a chapter in the book called One
is Greater Than Zero.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:50.810
It's like it'd be better just to train people
to do one job rather than zero,

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:54.960
which is what a lot of kids leave high school
or drop out of high school, are able to do.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.860
So what is to be done?

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:00.160
And you write in the concluding chapter of
the book,

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:01.660
"Slash government subsidies.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:03.680
This won't make classes relevant,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:07.260
but will lead students to spend fewer years
sitting in classrooms.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:12.560
Since they're not learning much of use, the
overarching effect will not be de-skilling,

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:14.220
but credential deflation."

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:15.340
Talk about that.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:19.080
Yeah, so credential inflation is what we've
seen over the last century.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:23.240
The amount of education that you need to get
one and the same job has increased dramatically.

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:25.960
So basically since World War II it'd be about
a four-year increase

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:28.020
in the amount of education you need to get
a job.

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:33.300
This is why we see college graduates doing
things like waiting tables, bartending,

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:35.140
where we're driving taxis.

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:39.411
These are not just ultra rare examples that
make it onto the news.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.860
These are common jobs for college graduates
to have these days,

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:46.440
and it does seem like even in these jobs,
a college degree does pay.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:51.700
It helps you get promoted and get a position
in the better restaurants or the better bars.

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:55.200
But the reason is there's so many people with
these degrees

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.120
that employers can afford to be picky and
say,

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:01.300
"Well, fine. I want college graduates tending my fancy
bar."

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:06.180
You know, like I say this is the function
of the proliferation of credentials.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:11.180
And if education were more expensive and the
subsidies were lower, fewer people would go.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.060
Now when you're talking about subsidies here,

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:19.460
I mean, you have 90 percent plus of K-12 spending
is spent on public schools.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:24.420
There's so much federal money and state money
going into colleges, as well even private colleges.

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:25.800
You're saying cut that?

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:26.440
Absolutely.

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:29.360
So cut government spending. Cut the subsidies.

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:34.440
This is the one of the most egregious cases
of industrial policy that we see all over the world,

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.340
and it's one that's almost totally uncontroversial

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.380
even though if you go into a classroom and
say,

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:42.100
"Well, wait, why are we going and teaching
these kids this stuff?

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:43.100
They're going to forget it anyway,

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:46.560
and it's not really relevant to what they're
going to do in real life, so why?"

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:52.040
And to save you from a charge of philistinism,
you're not saying in the book,

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:55.860
"Well, don't read novels, don't study art,
don't study music."

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:59.440
You're simply saying that as part of the curriculum,

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:03.240
most of what we learn other than basic mathematics
and literacy,

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.180
including English skills, is kind of useless.

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:08.080
Yeah, so useless in the labor market.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:12.510
Now, so you said I'm a professor and I am
a high culture kind of person

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.040
and German upright, Shakespeare.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.100
This is the stuff that I like.

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:20.690
But I still recognize that there's something
twisted about ramming it down a kid's throat.

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:26.020
A key part of appreciating this kind of stuff
is coming to it in your own good time,

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:28.580
actually being curious and ready.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:34.800
So like if you go and actually inspire a sincere
affection for Shakespeare or opera, that's great.

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.720
I see this happening almost never in school.

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:38.850
The kids are there.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:41.720
They go and pay lip service to it, and then
as soon as they're done,

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:44.900
they walk away and say, "Well, I never want
to have to hear that garbage again.

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:46.380
I hated that stuff."

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:50.660
This is where I say that while I'm not very
optimistic about the potential of online education

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:54.060
to really compete with brick and mortar schools,

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:58.220
it's already doing a tremendous job in terms
of quenching the human thirst for enlightenment.

00:19:58.220 --> 00:19:59.880
Right, and you need a course, and what's great
about the Internet

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:06.400
is that you don't have to concentrate all
your fire power on kids that are between 15 and 22.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.380
You can wait around for adults to say, "I'm
30.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:09.420
Now I'm curious about Shakespeare.

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:10.780
It seemed really boring when I was young,

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:14.420
but now maybe I would like to go and learn
something about it."

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:18.500
So, you know, to say that while I'm all on
board with the noble goals

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.060
of enlightening the human spirit,

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:24.060
a key part of this is you've got to have volunteers
that want to learn.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:25.880
Just trying to ram this down the throat of
conscripts,

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:28.240
which is what education normally does,

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:30.770
is an insult to everything that enlightenment
stands for.

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:31.520
You know what?

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:34.140
I'm hearing Pink Floyd's The Wall playing
in the background.

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:35.150
Yes, yes.

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:37.830
And this is the soundtrack of the book.

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:41.350
Bryan Caplan's latest book The Case Against
Education.

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:42.980
He's a George Mason economics professor.

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:46.640
Also you wrote a few years ago The Myth of
the Rational Voter.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:50.600
I think this book is going to be just as controversial
and hopefully as widely read.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:51.820
Bryan, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:53.420
Alright, thanks so much, always great talking to you, Nick.

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:55.160
For Reason, I'm Nick Gillespie.

